From 1722a9a506da8faa6ac0150efd6ae4d6936f4ad0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 23:58:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] libproc_macro: TokenStream::from_str lexes floats, tuple
 indices, escapes and raw identifiers

The lexer behind `TokenStream::from_str`/`Literal::from_str` (what `str::parse`,
`syn::parse_str` and proc-macro2 use inside a proc macro) had gaps rustc's lexer
doesn't:
- any digit followed by `.` hit `panic!("TODO: Floating point")`: no float
  literal could be parsed, nor `x.0.1`, `1.max(2)` or `1..2`. Numbers now follow
  mrustc's own lexer: an integer followed by `..`, or by `.` and an identifier,
  ends there; a number right after a single `.` is a tuple index (with its
  suffix, never a float); any other `.`, an exponent or an `f32`/`f64` suffix
  makes a float (`1f32` was the integer 1 followed by an identifier `f32`, `2e3`
  an integer and `e3`). Other suffixes stay on the literal as rustc's lexer
  allows (`10px` was an error).
- `'\u{..}'`, `'\x41'`, unknown char escapes and byte literals (`b'a'`) panicked;
  a byte string's `\x80`-`\xff` escapes became two UTF-8 bytes.
- `r#type` was `r` `#` `type`.
- the longest-symbol match recomputed its candidate range with mixed absolute
  and relative indices, so `<<=`, `>>=` and `...` came out as two symbols, and
  `..=` was missing.
- a `//` comment ending the input looped forever (`"x // c".parse()` hung), and
  an unterminated `/*` panicked instead of returning an error.

Two printing fixes go with the lexing, so printed literals parse back as what
they were: an unsuffixed float with a whole value printed as `1` (`syn::Lit`
then takes it for an integer) and now keeps `.0` as rustc's `f64_unsuffixed`
does, f32 literals print at f32 precision, and `'\\'` and backslashes in byte/C
strings are escaped. A raw identifier a proc macro creates (`Ident::new_raw`, or
`r#type` from the lexer) went to the compiler as the bare keyword; it is sent as
`r#type`, which the compiler already strips.

Needed before printing token streams the way rustc does (next commit): that
writes `self.0.clone()` and `x.1` without spaces, and a proc macro that parses
its own `to_string()` output would otherwise hit the float panic. Checked with
a stock rustc 1.90 building this crate: the new unit tests pass (the four
serialisation tests fail before and after), and 20000 random well-formed
streams print, lex back to the same tokens (spacing included) and reprint
identically; no input panics the lexer any more.

